Let $L,R$ be the two crossings, left and right, with the two end-pairs of the tangle NW,SW (at $L$) and NE,SE (at $R$). Let $a$ be the strand that is *over* at both crossings (the other case is the mirror image). Following $a$: SW $\to L\to$ upper edge of the lens $\to R\to$ SE; the other strand $b$: NW $\to L\to$ lower edge $\to R\to$ NE.

Using the rule "on the upper strand turn left for the 0-resolution":
* at $L$: the $0$-resolution joins SW with NW (a "vertical" cap) and the lens's upper and lower edges; the $1$-resolution joins NW with the lower edge and SW with the upper edge ("horizontal");
* at $R$: the $0$-resolution is horizontal (NE with the upper edge, SE with the lower edge), the $1$-resolution is vertical.

The four smoothings:

* $(0,0)$: $X$, in which NW–SW are joined and NE–SE are joined (no circle);
* $(1,1)$: the same diagram $X$;
* $(0,1)$: $X\sqcup\bigcirc$ (the lens becomes a small circle);
* $(1,0)$: $D'$, in which NW–NE and SW–SE are joined, i.e. the two strands pulled apart.

By Problem 2.3.1 (the coefficients are $A^{2},A^{-2},1,1$ respectively),
$$\langle D\rangle=A^2\langle X\rangle+A^{-2}\langle X\rangle+(-A^2-A^{-2})\langle X\rangle+\langle D'\rangle=\langle D'\rangle,$$
because the three terms in $\langle X\rangle$ cancel. (Here $\langle X\sqcup\bigcirc\rangle=(-A^2-A^{-2})\langle X\rangle$ by BP2; the rest of the diagram is the same in all four terms.) $\blacksquare$
